{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_sgqh6xb2psiap6wzytolwckayghjlqdl7uldcveney5xx3yrmapa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"That single reference is the `dlopen` path, confirming no Rust function is ever called directly. Verification is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe C library is one file (`c_src/src/lib.c`, 157 lines) exporting 5 symbols. `cargo check` passed with no errors from the start, and symbol parity was already complete — but the mandated phases surfaced a real bug that happy-path release testing had hidden.\n\n**Phase A artifacts** (in `translation/`):\n- `SYMBOLS.md` — all 5 C symbols (`safe_double_to_int`, `process_with_fallthrough`, `copy_data_block`, `handle_pointer_operations`, `overunder`); symbol diff **empty**, no stubs. Note that `include/lib.h` declares only `overunder`, but the other four have external linkage and are part of the ABI, so all five are tested directly.\n- `ERRORS.md` — 30 rows. This library has no error enum/`errno`/`assert`/null checks; its whole rejection surface is the three `safe_double_to_int` guards, the `default:` arm returning `-1`, the `strncpy` bound, and the unchecked `memcpy`.\n- `CONFIGS.md` — 41 rows over 12 axes derived from the source. No `[features]` section exists, so there is one build configuration.\n\n**Phases B/C** — 73 cases (43 + 30), all randomized with fixed seeds, comparing return values **and** captured `stdout` byte-for-byte via `dup2` on fd 1. Both `.so`s are loaded with `libloading`.\n\n**The bug I found and fixed** (`src/lib.rs`): `copy_data_block` used `core::ptr::copy_nonoverlapping`, whose *debug-only* preconditions (non-null, non-overlapping) made the Rust `.so` **panic** on two inputs the C accepts — `dest == src`, and NULL pointers. C dies with `SIGSEGV`; debug Rust aborted on a panic instead. Since `lib.c:78` calls `memcpy`, I changed both copy sites to call libc `memcpy`, restoring identical behavior in every profile. This was completely invisible in `--release`.\n\nTwo things worth flagging about my own process:\n\n1. **Four Phase C failures were my errors, not the code's.** I had hard-coded wrong fall-through sums (case 4 is +100 and case 3 is +60, not +130/+90), a wrong `handle_pointer_operations(INT_MAX)` value (98, not a saturated one), and a \"hand-verified\" overflow case (`a = d = 46341`) whose two wrapped halves sum back to a *positive* 9266. In all four, C and Rust already agreed. I fixed my expectations and propagated the corrections into `CONFIGS.md`/`ERRORS.md`.\n\n2. **An initial run showed 16 Phase B \"divergences\" that were not real** — libtest's multi-threaded progress output was leaking into the fd-1 captures. Rather than loosen the comparison, I switched both suites to `harness = false` with a sequential runner, which makes each capture exclusive. The library bytes had been identical all along.\n\n**Beyond the required scope:** because the C relies on signed-overflow wrap-around (UB) in several places, matching only the default unoptimized build would be weak evidence. I added a `DIFFTEST_C_SO` override and re-ran everything against the C rebuilt at `-O0/-O1/-O2/-O3/-Os` crossed with both Rust profiles — 20 extra combinations, all matching, without touching `c_src/`.\n\n`./run_all_configs.sh` reproduces the whole gate: **ALL CONFIGURATIONS PASSED** (6 feature×profile combinations, symbol diff empty in each). `c_src/` sources are unmodified."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":270,"cache_read_input_tokens":156648,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":270,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bcfbb187-1176-4d4d-b1a1-324dbbe6a964","uuid":"aaef58f4-7ca0-4298-9783-07c7589829d9","timestamp":"2026-08-28T05:22:34.041Z"}
